/**
 * @defgroup pump_head_calculator Pump Head Calculator
 * @ingroup pumping_system_assessments
 * @brief Determines the head developed by an operating pump from field measurements.
 * @details
 * Pump head is the mechanical energy that the pump adds to each unit weight of fluid, expressed as
 * feet of the pumped fluid. For steady, incompressible flow, that energy appears as changes in
 * elevation, pressure, and velocity and as energy dissipated between the measurement locations and
 * the pump flanges. The calculator combines those contributions to reconstruct the operating pump
 * head from field data. This approach follows the Pumping System Assessment Tool (PSAT) head-tool
 * method described in @cite casada2008psat.
 *
 * @heading{Final Result}
 * Total operating pump head is the sum of three signed differential heads and two estimated line
 * losses:
 * @formula{pump-head-total;
 *   H_{pump}=\Delta H_z+\Delta H_p+\Delta H_v+H_{f,s}+H_{f,d}
 * }
 * @symtable
 * @symrow{H_{pump}; Total operating pump head; \foot}
 * @symrow{\Delta H_z; Discharge-minus-suction elevation head; \foot}
 * @symrow{\Delta H_p; Discharge-minus-suction pressure head; \foot}
 * @symrow{\Delta H_v; Discharge-minus-suction velocity head; \foot}
 * @symrow{H_{f,s}; Estimated suction-line friction head; \foot}
 * @symrow{H_{f,d}; Estimated discharge-line friction head; \foot}
 * @endsymtable
 *
 * The three differential terms describe the change in fluid energy from the selected suction
 * reference point to the discharge gauge. Each may be positive, zero, or negative. The friction
 * terms are added because they restore the energy dissipated between the reference points and the
 * pump flanges. For nonnegative loss coefficients, both friction terms are nonnegative.
 *
 * @heading{Choose the Measurement Configuration}
 * Use the configuration that matches the available suction-side measurement:
 *
 * | Configuration | Suction reference point | Suction pressure | Suction velocity used in @math{\Delta H_v} |
 * |---|---|---|---|
 * | Suction gauge | Centerline of the suction pressure gauge | Measured suction gauge pressure @math{P_s} | Velocity in the suction pipe |
 * | Suction tank | Fluid surface in a tank, well, or reservoir | Gas overpressure above the fluid @math{P_t}; use 0 psig when open to atmosphere | Zero at the tank surface |
 *
 * In both configurations, the suction-pipe velocity is still calculated because it is needed for
 * the suction-line friction estimate. The tank assumption only sets the velocity at the tank fluid
 * surface to zero when calculating the differential velocity head.
 *
 * @heading{Measurements and Sign Convention}
 * - Measure flow in U.S. gallons per minute and pipe inside diameters in inches.
 * - Enter pressures as gauge pressure in psig. Vacuum on the suction side may therefore be entered
 *   as a negative gauge pressure.
 * - Reference every elevation to the same datum. The datum may be arbitrary, such as a plant floor,
 *   because only elevation differences are used.
 * - Use one steady flow rate for the suction and discharge pipes.
 * - Enter fluid specific gravity relative to the reference water used by the pressure-head
 *   conversion.
 * - Sum the applicable dimensionless loss coefficients separately for the suction and discharge
 *   paths between their reference points and the pump flanges.
 *
 * The calculation proceeds from the field measurements as follows:
 * 1. Convert flow to cubic feet per second and pipe diameters to feet.
 * 2. Calculate suction- and discharge-pipe velocities and velocity heads.
 * 3. Calculate the elevation, pressure, and velocity differences for the selected configuration.
 * 4. Estimate suction- and discharge-line friction heads from their loss coefficients.
 * 5. Sum all five contributions using \eqref{eq:pump-head-total}.
 *
 * @heading{Elevation Head}
 * Elevation head is the vertical change from the suction reference point to the discharge gauge:
 * @formula{pump-head-elevation;
 *   \Delta H_z=\begin{cases}
 *     z_d-z_s & \text{suction gauge}\\
 *     z_d-z_t & \text{suction tank}
 *   \end{cases}
 * }
 * @symtable
 * @symrow{\Delta H_z; Discharge-minus-suction elevation head; \foot}
 * @symrow{z_d; Discharge-gauge centerline elevation; \foot}
 * @symrow{z_s; Suction-gauge centerline elevation; \foot}
 * @symrow{z_t; Suction-tank fluid-surface elevation; \foot}
 * @endsymtable
 *
 * A discharge gauge below the suction reference point produces a negative elevation-head
 * contribution. This is physically valid and reduces the head the pump must develop.
 *
 * @heading{Pressure Head}
 * Pressure head follows from the hydrostatic relation @math{H=p/\gamma}. Pressure in psi is first
 * multiplied by 144 to express it per square foot. Fluid specific weight is represented as the
 * reference-water specific weight multiplied by specific gravity:
 * @formula{pump-head-pressure-basis;
 *   \gamma=SG\,\gamma_w,\qquad
 *   k_p=\frac{\gamma_w}{144},\qquad
 *   \Delta H_p=\frac{144\,\Delta P}{\gamma_w SG}=\frac{\Delta P}{k_p SG}
 * }
 * @symtable
 * @symrow{\gamma; Specific weight of the pumped fluid; \pound\per\cubicFoot}
 * @symrow{\gamma_w; Reference-water specific weight represented by the calculator, 62.316000144; \pound\per\cubicFoot}
 * @symrow{SG; Fluid specific gravity relative to the reference water; \unitless}
 * @symrow{k_p; Reference-water pressure gradient, 0.432750001; \psi\per\foot}
 * @symrow{144; Square inches per square foot; \squareInch\per\squareFoot}
 * @symrow{\Delta P; Discharge-minus-suction gauge-pressure difference; \psi}
 * @symrow{\Delta H_p; Discharge-minus-suction pressure head; \foot}
 * @endsymtable
 *
 * Apply the pressure difference for the selected configuration:
 * @formula{pump-head-pressure-configurations;
 *   \Delta P=\begin{cases}
 *     P_d-P_s & \text{suction gauge}\\
 *     P_d-P_t & \text{suction tank}
 *   \end{cases}
 * }
 * @symtable
 * @symrow{\Delta P; Discharge-minus-suction gauge-pressure difference; \psi}
 * @symrow{P_d; Discharge gauge pressure; \psig}
 * @symrow{P_s; Suction gauge pressure; \psig}
 * @symrow{P_t; Suction-tank gas overpressure; \psig}
 * @endsymtable
 *
 * The retained pressure gradient is equivalent to 2.310803 feet of water per psi at
 * @math{SG=1}. DOE pump guidance commonly explains this relationship with the engineering
 * approximation 2.31 feet per psi and a water specific weight of approximately 62.3 pounds-force
 * per cubic foot @cite doe2000energyMatters.
 *
 * @heading{Pipe Velocity and Velocity Head}
 * Convert the entered flow and diameter before applying continuity and the velocity-head relation:
 * @formula{pump-head-flow-conversion;
 *   Q=\frac{q}{k_Q},\qquad D_{ft}=\frac{D}{12}
 * }
 * @symtable
 * @symrow{Q; Volumetric flow rate used by the velocity calculation; \cubicFoot\per\second}
 * @symrow{q; Entered volumetric flow rate; \gallon\per\minute}
 * @symrow{k_Q; Retained flow conversion, 448.8311693; \gallon\second\per\minute\cubicFoot}
 * @symrow{D_{ft}; Pipe inside diameter used by the area calculation; \foot}
 * @symrow{D; Entered pipe inside diameter; \inch}
 * @symrow{12; Inches per foot; \inch\per\foot}
 * @endsymtable
 *
 * @formula{pump-head-velocity;
 *   A=\frac{\pi D_{ft}^{2}}{4},\qquad
 *   v=\frac{Q}{A},\qquad
 *   H_v=\frac{v^2}{2g}
 * }
 * @symtable
 * @symrow{A; Internal pipe cross-sectional area; \squareFoot}
 * @symrow{\pi; Circle ratio; \unitless}
 * @symrow{D_{ft}; Pipe inside diameter; \foot}
 * @symrow{v; Average pipe velocity; \foot\per\second}
 * @symrow{Q; Volumetric flow rate; \cubicFoot\per\second}
 * @symrow{H_v; Pipe velocity head; \foot}
 * @symrow{g; Gravitational acceleration, 32.174; \foot\per\second\squared}
 * @symrow{4; Geometric denominator for circular area expressed with diameter; \unitless}
 * @symrow{2; Kinetic-energy denominator in the velocity-head relation; \unitless}
 * @endsymtable
 *
 * The differential velocity head is then:
 * @formula{pump-head-velocity-differential;
 *   \Delta H_v=\begin{cases}
 *     H_{v,d}-H_{v,s} & \text{suction gauge}\\
 *     H_{v,d} & \text{suction tank}
 *   \end{cases}
 * }
 * @symtable
 * @symrow{\Delta H_v; Discharge-minus-suction velocity head; \foot}
 * @symrow{H_{v,d}; Discharge-pipe velocity head; \foot}
 * @symrow{H_{v,s}; Suction-pipe velocity head; \foot}
 * @endsymtable
 *
 * Equal suction and discharge pipe diameters give equal pipe velocities and therefore zero
 * differential velocity head in the suction-gauge configuration.
 *
 * @heading{Estimated Line Losses}
 * Each aggregate loss coefficient multiplies the velocity head in the pipe used as that side's
 * reference diameter:
 * @formula{pump-head-friction;
 *   H_{f,s}=K_sH_{v,s},\qquad H_{f,d}=K_dH_{v,d}
 * }
 * @symtable
 * @symrow{H_{f,s}; Estimated suction-line friction head; \foot}
 * @symrow{H_{f,d}; Estimated discharge-line friction head; \foot}
 * @symrow{K_s; Aggregate suction-line loss coefficient; \unitless}
 * @symrow{K_d; Aggregate discharge-line loss coefficient; \unitless}
 * @symrow{H_{v,s}; Suction-pipe velocity head; \foot}
 * @symrow{H_{v,d}; Discharge-pipe velocity head; \foot}
 * @endsymtable
 *
 * A fitting coefficient tabulated for a different diameter must be normalized to the pipe diameter
 * used by the calculator. For the same flow, velocity varies with @math{1/D^2} and velocity head
 * varies with @math{1/D^4}. Therefore:
 * @formula{pump-head-loss-coefficient-scaling;
 *   K_{ref}=K_e\left(\frac{D_{ref}}{D_e}\right)^4
 * }
 * @symtable
 * @symrow{K_{ref}; Loss coefficient normalized to the calculator's reference pipe; \unitless}
 * @symrow{K_e; Loss coefficient stated for the fitting or element; \unitless}
 * @symrow{D_{ref}; Inside diameter of the reference pipe entered in the calculator; \inch}
 * @symrow{D_e; Inside diameter associated with the stated element coefficient; \inch}
 * @endsymtable
 *
 * For example, a 12-inch valve with @math{K_e=0.4} located in a system referenced to a 16-inch
 * pipe contributes @math{K_{ref}=0.4(16/12)^4=1.26}. This normalization guidance and example are
 * consistent with the PSAT head-tool documentation @cite casada2008psat.
 *
 * @heading{Unit Conversions and Reference Properties}
 * The flow conversion is a unit relationship. One cubic foot contains 1728 cubic inches, one U.S.
 * liquid gallon contains exactly 231 cubic inches, and one minute contains 60 seconds
 * @cite chisholm1967units. Thus:
 * @formula{pump-head-gpm-per-cfs;
 *   k_{Q,exact}=\frac{1728}{231}(60)=448.831168831\ldots
 * }
 * @symtable
 * @symrow{k_{Q,exact}; Unit-derived gallons per minute in one cubic foot per second; \gallon\second\per\minute\cubicFoot}
 * @symrow{1728; Cubic inches per cubic foot; \inch\cubed\per\cubicFoot}
 * @symrow{231; Cubic inches per U.S. liquid gallon; \inch\cubed\per\gallon}
 * @symrow{60; Seconds per minute; \second\per\minute}
 * @endsymtable
 *
 * The implementation retains 448.8311693 rather than replacing it with the longer unit-derived
 * value so existing calculation results remain unchanged. See
 * @ref physics::conversions::kGallonsPerMinutePerCubicFootPerSecond.
 *
 * The pressure factor is a reference property rather than a pure unit conversion. The retained
 * 0.432750001 psi per foot represents the chosen reference-water pressure gradient. It implies
 * @math{\gamma_w=144(0.432750001)=62.316000144} pounds-force per cubic foot and the reciprocal
 * 2.310803 feet per psi. Its exact historical source and precision are not recorded, so the value is
 * preserved as a compatibility constant rather than presented as a universal water property. See
 * @ref physics::conversions::kPsiPerFootOfWater.
 *
 * @heading{Worked Suction-Gauge Example}
 * Consider water-like fluid with @math{SG=1.0} flowing at 2000 gpm. The suction measurements are
 * @math{D_s=17.9} inches, @math{P_s=5} psig, @math{z_s=5} feet, and @math{K_s=1}. The discharge
 * measurements are @math{D_d=15} inches, @math{P_d=50} psig, @math{z_d=1} foot, and
 * @math{K_d=1}.
 *
 * | Contribution | Calculation result (ft) | Interpretation |
 * |---|---:|---|
 * | @math{\Delta H_z} | -4.000000 | The discharge gauge is 4 ft below the suction gauge. |
 * | @math{\Delta H_p} | 103.986135 | The pressure rise is the dominant contribution. |
 * | @math{\Delta H_v} | 0.103859 | The smaller discharge pipe has the greater velocity head. |
 * | @math{H_{f,s}} | 0.101040 | Estimated loss from the suction gauge to the pump. |
 * | @math{H_{f,d}} | 0.204899 | Estimated loss from the pump to the discharge gauge. |
 * | **@math{H_{pump}}** | **100.395932** | Sum of all five contributions. |
 *
 * The negative elevation term does not indicate an error; it offsets part of the pressure-head rise.
 *
 * @heading{WebAssembly Example}
 * The package exports typed input and result objects from its root entrypoint. The `locateFile`
 * path depends on where the consuming application deploys `client.wasm`.
 *
 * @code{.ts}
 * import createModule, {
 *   type MeasurToolsSuite,
 *   type PumpHeadSuctionGaugeInput,
 * } from 'measur-tools-suite';
 *
 * const suite: MeasurToolsSuite = await createModule({
 *   locateFile: (filename: string) => `/path/to/wasm/${filename}`,
 * });
 *
 * const input: PumpHeadSuctionGaugeInput = {
 *   specificGravity: 1.0,
 *   flowRate: 2000,
 *   suctionPipeDiameter: 17.9,
 *   suctionGaugePressure: 5,
 *   suctionGaugeElevation: 5,
 *   suctionLineLossCoefficients: 1,
 *   dischargePipeDiameter: 15,
 *   dischargeGaugePressure: 50,
 *   dischargeGaugeElevation: 1,
 *   dischargeLineLossCoefficients: 1,
 * };
 *
 * const result = suite.calculatePumpHeadFromSuctionGauge(input);
 * console.log(result.pumpHead); // approximately 100.396 ft
 * @endcode
 *
 * For a tank-surface reference, provide a `PumpHeadSuctionTankInput` and call
 * `calculatePumpHeadFromSuctionTank`.
 *
 * @heading{Assumptions and Limits}
 * - Flow is steady and incompressible, and the same volumetric flow passes through both pipes.
 * - Pipe diameters are inside diameters. Specific gravity is constant over the calculation.
 * - Pressure measurements are gauge pressures and elevations share a common datum.
 * - The suction tank or reservoir is large enough that fluid velocity at its surface is negligible.
 * - Loss coefficients are supplied by the user, are normalized to the entered reference-pipe
 *   diameters, and represent only the paths between the reference points and pump flanges.
 * - The calculation does not enforce physical input ranges. Specific gravity and pipe diameters
 *   must be greater than zero, and users must determine whether the measurements and coefficients
 *   are suitable for the system being assessed.
 * - This calculation determines operating pump head; it does not calculate net positive suction
 *   head, pump efficiency, cavitation margin, transient behavior, or a complete system curve.
 *
 * @heading{Related Implementation}
 * - C++ namespace: @ref pump_head
 * - Suction-gauge calculation: @ref pump_head::calculateFromSuctionGauge
 * - Suction-tank calculation: @ref pump_head::calculateFromSuctionTank
 * - Shared constants: @ref physics::conversions and @ref physics::us
 */
